WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.150
Across the mouth of the Humber, a
finger of sand reaches out: Spurn.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.130
It is over 3 miles long, but
in places only 50 metres wide.

3
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:15.150
In December 2013, a tidal surge burst
through its narrowest part and destroyed

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.350
the road.

5
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.750
The British Geological Survey says
modelled scenarios predict rising seas will

6
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.650
cut off Spurn Head by 2050.

7
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:27.750
So how did a strip of sand this
thin form across a river mouth?

8
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.870
Did the Humber dump it there?

9
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.530
In this mission, you'll watch cliffs
retreat and headlands break into caves,

10
00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.950
arches, stacks and stumps.

11
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.670
Then you'll walk a dune transect,
from the strandline to fixed dunes.

12
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:46.030
Your mission: explain how Spurn
formed across the mouth of the Humber.
